1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,219
There are places in the
universe where gravity becomes

2
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:12,724
so strong that nothing can
escape, not even light.

3
00:00:12,804 --> 00:00:14,994
We call them black holes.

4
00:00:15,144 --> 00:00:17,937
They are not empty voids,
and they do not roam

5
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:21,323
the cosmos swallowing
everything in their path.

6
00:00:21,403 --> 00:00:25,560
They are objects with enormous
mass squeezed into a tiny space,

7
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:30,517
and they bend the world around them
in ways that still astonish us.

8
00:00:30,597 --> 00:00:33,417
A black hole can be
born from a dying star,

9
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:36,903
or grow to weigh as much
as billions of suns.

10
00:00:36,983 --> 00:00:40,155
We cannot see one directly,
yet we have weighed them,

11
00:00:40,235 --> 00:00:43,918
listened to them, and even
photographed their shadows.

12
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:48,815
This is how we learned to find the
darkest objects in the universe.

13
00:00:51,165 --> 00:00:54,815
Every star lives in a
balance between two forces.

14
00:00:54,895 --> 00:00:57,706
Gravity pulls inward, trying to crush it.

15
00:00:57,786 --> 00:01:02,427
The heat and pressure from nuclear
fusion in its core push outward.

16
00:01:02,507 --> 00:01:07,748
For millions or billions of years,
the two forces cancel each other out.

17
00:01:07,828 --> 00:01:12,598
But when a very massive star runs
out of fuel, the balance breaks.

18
00:01:12,678 --> 00:01:15,639
Its core collapses in
a fraction of a second,

19
00:01:15,719 --> 00:01:19,535
and the outer layers are
blown away in a supernova.

20
00:01:19,615 --> 00:01:23,291
If what remains is heavy enough,
roughly more than three

21
00:01:23,371 --> 00:01:27,563
times the mass of the Sun,
nothing can stop the collapse.

22
00:01:27,643 --> 00:01:31,145
The core keeps shrinking,
and a black hole is born.

23
00:01:31,225 --> 00:01:34,727
Astronomers estimate that
our own galaxy may hold as

24
00:01:34,807 --> 00:01:38,966
many as a hundred million of
these stellar black holes.

25
00:01:39,046 --> 00:01:43,419
Most are invisible,
drifting quietly among the stars.

26
00:01:45,269 --> 00:01:50,070
Around every black hole is a
boundary called the event horizon.

27
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:54,855
It is not a surface you could
touch. It is the point of no return.

28
00:01:54,935 --> 00:01:58,741
Inside it, the escape speed is
greater than the speed of light,

29
00:01:58,821 --> 00:02:01,720
and nothing can travel faster than light.

30
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:05,847
So whatever crosses the horizon
can never come back out.

31
00:02:05,927 --> 00:02:09,294
The size of the horizon
depends on the mass.

32
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:15,110
A black hole with the mass of our Sun
would be only about six kilometers across.

33
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:19,662
And here is a surprise: if the Sun
were suddenly replaced by a black

34
00:02:19,742 --> 00:02:24,395
hole of the same mass, Earth would
keep orbiting just as before.

35
00:02:24,475 --> 00:02:29,685
Gravity depends on mass and distance,
not on what the mass is made of.

36
00:02:29,765 --> 00:02:33,008
The danger comes only
when you get very close.

37
00:02:33,088 --> 00:02:35,728
There, gravity pulls much harder on the

38
00:02:35,808 --> 00:02:38,797
near side of an object
than on the far side,

39
00:02:38,877 --> 00:02:45,036
stretching it into a long thin strand, a
process physicists call spaghettification.

40
00:02:45,116 --> 00:02:49,093
Time itself slows near a black
hole. Seen from far away,

41
00:02:49,173 --> 00:02:51,992
a clock falling toward the horizon would

42
00:02:52,072 --> 00:02:55,702
tick slower and slower,
and appear to freeze.

43
00:02:55,782 --> 00:02:58,721
At the center, theory
predicts a singularity,

44
00:02:58,801 --> 00:03:02,957
a point where our known laws
of physics stop working.

45
00:03:05,307 --> 00:03:09,161
If black holes give off no
light, how do we find them?

46
00:03:09,361 --> 00:03:12,044
By watching what happens around them.

47
00:03:12,194 --> 00:03:17,180
When a black hole has a companion star,
it can pull gas away from it.

48
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:21,300
That gas does not fall straight
in. It spirals into a flat,

49
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:24,544
spinning disk, called an accretion disk.

50
00:03:24,624 --> 00:03:27,874
Friction heats the disk
to millions of degrees,

51
00:03:27,954 --> 00:03:31,228
so hot that it shines brightly in X-rays.

52
00:03:31,308 --> 00:03:36,317
One of the first strong candidates
was an X-ray source in the

53
00:03:36,397 --> 00:03:41,939
constellation Cygnus, known as
Cygnus X-1, identified in 1971.

54
00:03:42,019 --> 00:03:44,948
It is about seven
thousand light-years away.

55
00:03:45,028 --> 00:03:49,394
NASA's Chandra X-ray Observatory,
launched in 1999,

56
00:03:49,474 --> 00:03:53,592
has since found black
holes across the sky.

57
00:03:53,672 --> 00:03:59,160
Sometimes, a black hole does something
even more dramatic: it launches jets.

58
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:04,121
Twisted magnetic fields near the
horizon can blast part of the infalling

59
00:04:04,201 --> 00:04:09,669
matter outward as beams of particles,
moving at nearly the speed of light.

60
00:04:09,749 --> 00:04:13,628
A black hole is not only a
place where things disappear. It

61
00:04:13,708 --> 00:04:18,401
can also be one of the most
powerful engines in the universe.

62
00:04:20,251 --> 00:04:26,085
In 1915, Albert Einstein published
his theory of general relativity.

63
00:04:26,165 --> 00:04:32,472
It describes gravity not as a force, but
as the bending of space and time by mass.

64
00:04:32,552 --> 00:04:36,500
It also predicted that when
massive objects accelerate,

65
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:41,563
they send ripples across spacetime,
called gravitational waves.

66
00:04:41,643 --> 00:04:45,433
Two black holes circling each
other lose energy as waves,

67
00:04:45,513 --> 00:04:49,006
and spiral closer and
closer until they merge.

68
00:04:49,086 --> 00:04:54,024
On September 14, 2015,
detectors of the LIGO observatory

69
00:04:54,104 --> 00:04:59,761
in the United States felt such
a ripple for the first time.

70
00:04:59,841 --> 00:05:06,109
It came from two black holes, about
36 and 29 times the mass of the Sun,

71
00:05:06,189 --> 00:05:09,909
that had merged 1.3 billion years ago.

72
00:05:09,989 --> 00:05:14,031
The whole signal lasted
only a fraction of a second.

73
00:05:14,111 --> 00:05:16,972
In that instant,
about three times the mass

74
00:05:17,052 --> 00:05:20,090
of the Sun was turned into pure energy.

75
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:25,816
For a fraction of a second, the merger
radiated more power than all the stars in

76
00:05:25,896 --> 00:05:28,178
the observable universe combined,

77
00:05:28,258 --> 00:05:32,207
almost all of it as invisible
gravitational waves.

78
00:05:32,287 --> 00:05:35,873
A new way of observing
the universe had begun.

79
00:05:38,223 --> 00:05:41,697
Not all black holes are
born from single stars.

80
00:05:41,777 --> 00:05:46,936
At the center of almost every large
galaxy sits a supermassive black hole,

81
00:05:47,016 --> 00:05:51,492
weighing millions or billions
of times as much as the Sun.

82
00:05:51,572 --> 00:05:57,763
Ours is called Sagittarius A-star,
about 26,000 light-years from Earth.

83
00:05:57,843 --> 00:06:00,587
It weighs about four million Suns.

84
00:06:00,737 --> 00:06:03,442
We know this because
for almost thirty years,

85
00:06:03,522 --> 00:06:06,554
astronomers have tracked
stars orbiting it.

86
00:06:06,634 --> 00:06:11,256
One of them, named S2, circles
the center every sixteen years,

87
00:06:11,336 --> 00:06:16,407
racing past at more than seven
thousand kilometers per second.

88
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:19,773
Only something extremely
massive and extremely

89
00:06:19,853 --> 00:06:23,224
compact can hold a star on such an orbit.

90
00:06:23,304 --> 00:06:27,640
In 2020, the Nobel Prize in
Physics honored this discovery,

91
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,757
together with the theory
of how black holes form.

92
00:06:31,837 --> 00:06:35,920
Then, in 2019, a global
network of radio telescopes,

93
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,294
called the Event Horizon Telescope,
did something once thought impossible.

94
00:06:42,374 --> 00:06:45,889
It captured the first
image of a black hole,

95
00:06:45,969 --> 00:06:50,751
in the galaxy Messier 87,
55 million light-years away.

96
00:06:50,831 --> 00:06:56,239
The glowing ring surrounds a dark shadow,
the silhouette of the horizon itself.

97
00:06:56,319 --> 00:07:00,156
That black hole weighs about
six and a half billion Suns.

98
00:07:00,236 --> 00:07:05,674
In 2022, the same team showed us
the shadow of Sagittarius A-star,

99
00:07:05,754 --> 00:07:08,716
at the heart of our own galaxy.

100
00:07:08,796 --> 00:07:12,718
And the jet of M87 stretches
thousands of light-years,

101
00:07:12,798 --> 00:07:18,066
a beam of particles visible even
to the Hubble Space Telescope.

102
00:07:20,416 --> 00:07:25,780
Black holes do not only bend space.
They bend the paths of light itself.

103
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:29,062
This effect is called
gravitational lensing,

104
00:07:29,142 --> 00:07:31,747
and any massive object can cause it,

105
00:07:31,827 --> 00:07:35,926
from a single star to a
whole cluster of galaxies.

106
00:07:36,006 --> 00:07:39,168
The light from a distant
galaxy gets stretched into

107
00:07:39,248 --> 00:07:43,176
arcs and rings around the
massive object in front of it.

108
00:07:43,256 --> 00:07:47,290
Astronomers use these natural
magnifying glasses to see

109
00:07:47,370 --> 00:07:51,703
galaxies that would otherwise
be too faint to detect.

110
00:07:51,783 --> 00:07:55,319
Near a black hole,
the effect becomes extreme.

111
00:07:55,399 --> 00:07:59,100
Light from the disk behind it
is bent around the horizon,

112
00:07:59,180 --> 00:08:05,085
so we can see the far side of the disk
appearing above and below the black hole.

113
00:08:06,935 --> 00:08:11,175
Perhaps the biggest surprise is
how early black holes appear.

114
00:08:11,255 --> 00:08:15,491
The James Webb Space Telescope
has found black holes that already

115
00:08:15,571 --> 00:08:19,859
existed within a few hundred
million years of the Big Bang.

116
00:08:19,939 --> 00:08:25,567
One of them, found together with NASA's
Chandra, was already millions of times the

117
00:08:25,647 --> 00:08:31,516
mass of the Sun when the universe was
less than five hundred million years old.

118
00:08:31,596 --> 00:08:33,989
How could they grow so fast?

119
00:08:34,189 --> 00:08:38,001
Maybe they were born large,
from collapsing clouds of gas.

120
00:08:38,081 --> 00:08:42,275
Maybe they fed on matter faster
than we thought possible.

121
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:44,578
Scientists do not know yet.

122
00:08:44,728 --> 00:08:49,662
That is what makes this one of the
liveliest questions in astronomy today.

123
00:08:51,512 --> 00:08:57,719
In 1974, Stephen Hawking showed that
black holes are not completely black.

124
00:08:57,799 --> 00:09:00,737
Because of quantum
effects near the horizon,

125
00:09:00,817 --> 00:09:06,333
a black hole should slowly leak a faint
glow, now called Hawking radiation.

126
00:09:06,413 --> 00:09:11,013
For a black hole as heavy as a star,
this glow is unimaginably weak,

127
00:09:11,093 --> 00:09:16,350
and it would take far longer than
the age of the universe to fade away.

128
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:21,782
No one has ever detected Hawking
radiation. It remains a prediction.

129
00:09:21,862 --> 00:09:23,610
And it raises a deep puzzle:

130
00:09:23,690 --> 00:09:28,336
what happens to the information
about everything that fell in?

131
00:09:28,536 --> 00:09:31,616
Physicists are still
working on the answer.

132
00:09:33,466 --> 00:09:35,807
Is there a black hole near us?

133
00:09:36,007 --> 00:09:41,209
One of the closest we know of is about
sixteen hundred light-years away,

134
00:09:41,289 --> 00:09:46,427
found with data from the European
Space Agency's Gaia spacecraft.

135
00:09:46,507 --> 00:09:49,968
Our Sun will never become
one. It is far too light,

136
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:53,542
and will end its life
as a quiet white dwarf.

137
00:09:53,622 --> 00:09:56,455
Black holes are not monsters in the dark.

138
00:09:56,535 --> 00:10:00,924
They are a natural result of gravity,
and they shape galaxies,

139
00:10:01,004 --> 00:10:04,761
power jets, and send
ripples across the cosmos.

140
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:07,990
We found them by looking at
what they do to the light,

141
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:10,891
the matter, and space itself around them.

142
00:10:10,971 --> 00:10:13,878
The darkest objects in
the universe have taught

143
00:10:13,958 --> 00:10:17,227
us some of the brightest ideas in science.
